<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      The cluster-backed half of the shared provider conformance suite, run against the
      managed-PostgreSQL provider.

      ⚠ THIS PROJECT WAS MISSING FOR THE SECOND PROVIDER FAMILY IN THE TREE, AND ITS ABSENCE WAS
      GREEN. CyberCloud.Providers.DBforPostgreSQL.Conformance has carried
      `PostgresServerClusterConformance : ClusterBackedConformanceTests` since the family landed, and
      that class's one test skips with a message promising that the five cluster-backed criteria "run
      in … its own <Provider>.Cluster.Conformance project". Fourteen families had one; this family and
      CyberCloud.Communication did not, and Communication says so by name in its own conformance
      project. Nothing counts `.Cluster.Conformance` projects against `ClusterBackedConformanceTests`
      registrations, so the promise stood unkept until 2026-09-17, when every cluster-backed suite was
      run for real for the first time and the table of them had no PostgreSQL row. This is the row.

      ⚠ IT CONTAINS NO ASSERTIONS AND NO INFRASTRUCTURE. Everything it runs comes from
      test/CyberCloud.Cluster.Conformance; what lives here is two class declarations over the SAME
      ProviderConformanceCase the Docker-free suite already uses, plus one AssemblyInfo line.

      ⚠ IT IS A SEPARATE PROJECT FROM CyberCloud.Providers.DBforPostgreSQL.Conformance, AND THAT IS
      THE WHOLE SPLIT. That project must keep running on a machine with no Docker — it is the per-PR
      gate for every provider change, and Build.Architecture § LabelsGate points at it. Adding
      Testcontainers to it would make the twelve-step write path, the verb grammar, the four
      reconciler clauses and the cross-tenant 404 all unrunnable without a daemon.

      ⚠ IT IS NOT NAMED `.Conformance.Cluster`. Directory.Build.props § Project role detection and
      build/Build.Test.cs § SuiteOwning both classify by the SUFFIX of the project name; a name ending
      in `.Cluster` is claimed by neither, so the project would build as a library rather than as a
      test host.

      ⚠ WHAT IT PROVES FOR THIS PROVIDER AND CANNOT PROVE. The k3s it starts has no CloudNativePG, so
      what is exercised is the apply path for the Cluster and the Pooler, ADR-013's label injection
      under real admission, the refusal a missing CRD produces, drift after a real delete, a conflict
      with another field manager, the durable round trip and the silo kill. Everything that depends on
      the operator running — the primary starting, the generated `{name}-app` Secret, listKeys
      answering — is test/CyberCloud.Bundle.Cluster.Conformance § M1StoryOnAFreshCluster's, which
      installs the operator first; charts/managed/postgres/conformance.yaml § owed keeps the rest.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Shouldly"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="Global usings">
        <Using Include="Xunit"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
          ⚠ The CASE comes from the provider's Docker-free conformance project rather than being
          declared again here. One provider, one ProviderConformanceCase: a second copy would be a
          second description of the same provider, and the two would disagree the first time either
          changed.
        -->
        <ProjectReference
            Include="../CyberCloud.Providers.DBforPostgreSQL.Conformance/CyberCloud.Providers.DBforPostgreSQL.Conformance.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../../test/CyberCloud.Cluster.Conformance/CyberCloud.Cluster.Conformance.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
